WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:07.049
Did you know that one dollar actually equals one cent?

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:13.409
I know what you're thinking: you're going to tell me that one dollar is equal to 100 cents.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:21.539
But mathematically, 100 is equal to 10 squared so this is equal to 10 cents squared.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.980
Therefore this equals to 0.1 dollar squared,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.000
and since mathematically 0.1 squared is equal to .01,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.100
this is equal to $.01. And point zero one dollars is 1/100 of a dollar which is exactly equal to one cent.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.380
So there you go: one dollar is exactly equal to one cent.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.400
And by this logic,

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:58.460
Verizon finally has a proof that $.002 is equal to point .002 cents.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.460
Now, of course, this is just an April Fool's joke.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.900
This false proof comes from the most common errors in undergraduate mathematics,

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.919
written by Eric Schechter who's a professor emeritus of the math department at Vanderbilt University.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.019
And no doubt you'll be able to see where the mistake is in this false proof,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:25.049
because every single math teacher has seen a student submit a proof with this kind of error.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.810
Can you figure it out?

